Use Wine's QPC clock domain for native movie scheduling.

Wine 10 MCIAVI_player schedules its video with QueryPerformanceCounter.
NtQueryPerformanceCounter's Unix monotonic_counter prefers
CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW and falls back to CLOCK_MONOTONIC. Native movies used
CLOCK_MONOTONIC unconditionally; its adjusted rate can differ from QPC.

Use the same successful RAW/otherwise MONOTONIC selection. Device playback
and producer scheduling retain their independent clocks. Millisecond
precision, browser movie timing and complete original A/V parity remain
separate requirements; no clock offset or output is fitted.

Primary source:
https://github.com/wine-mirror/wine/blob/wine-10.0/dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c

--- a/dd2_movie_platform.c
+++ b/dd2_movie_platform.c
@@ -76,7 +76,13 @@
 #ifdef DD2_BROWSER
     return movie_clock();
 #else
-    struct timespec ts;clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC,&ts);
+    struct timespec ts;
+    /* Wine 10 NtQueryPerformanceCounter prefers the unadjusted clock.
+     * MCIAVI schedules video with QPC, separately from its audio device. */
+#ifdef CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW
+    if(clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW,&ts))
+#endif
+        clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC,&ts);
     return (unsigned)((uint64_t)ts.tv_sec*1000u+ts.tv_nsec/1000000u);
 #endif
 }
